1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Engines freeze. Frostbite consumes limbs.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
Death in the extreme cove is just a mistake away.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Yet, a small city thrives atop 9,000 feet of ice.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
A probe hurdles through the blistering cold of deep space.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
A ship opens a lifeline in the frozen sea.

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
Now, Sub-Zero on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,680
If you're able to withstand its icy grip,

8
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:42,280
the cold can be a gateway to both material and spiritual enrichment.

9
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:47,040
From the billions of barrels of oil pouring out of the north shore of Alaska

10
00:00:47,041 --> 00:00:51,060
to communities accustomed to minus 40 degrees Fahrenheit.

11
00:00:51,520 --> 00:00:55,700
It's just living in a place that automatically challenges you

12
00:00:55,701 --> 00:00:59,420
and is frankly never very boring.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:00,800
To science.

14
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:06,160
When you dig down into the ice, you'll be looking at ice that was formed a thousand years ago,

15
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:09,720
or a hundred thousand years ago, or five hundred thousand years ago.

16
00:01:10,160 --> 00:01:11,020
To recreation.

17
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:14,580
Cold temperatures are exhilarating.

18
00:01:14,581 --> 00:01:16,620
You're pushed to the edge.

19
00:01:17,520 --> 00:01:20,040
You've got to be focused all the time.

20
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:22,480
You make a mistake, you're going to pay.

21
00:01:24,180 --> 00:01:28,180
Shelter in these cold and often barren locales has always been a challenge.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,160
Few places on Earth offer as many obstacles to building and sustaining a structure as the South Pole.

23
00:01:39,020 --> 00:01:45,660
The geographic South Pole, the bottom of the world, sits atop more than 9,000 feet of snow and ice.

24
00:01:47,480 --> 00:01:52,100
Temperatures during the austral winter can dip to minus 117 degrees Fahrenheit.

25
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:58,740
And the ice beneath your feet is literally moving, drifting 33 feet a year.

26
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:04,120
So why build anything at the coldest, most remote locale on Earth?

27
00:02:05,980 --> 00:02:09,840
Turns out that because it's so dry and it's 10,000 feet elevation,

28
00:02:10,700 --> 00:02:15,240
South Pole Station is one of the world's best places for certain kinds of astrophysics.

29
00:02:15,800 --> 00:02:19,520
Particularly study looking back in time at the early moments of the universe,

30
00:02:19,760 --> 00:02:22,100
looking at the afterglow from the Big Bang.

31
00:02:23,580 --> 00:02:27,360
Research at the South Pole has included everything from global climate study

32
00:02:27,361 --> 00:02:29,640
to rover testing for Mars missions.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:43,430
Since a multinational study of the solar and terrestrial environment, known as the International Geophysical Year of 1957-1958, the United States has maintained a

34
00:02:43,431 --> 00:02:46,000
science base at the South Pole.

35
00:02:46,001 --> 00:02:54,000
It's an ideal place for science, but it's a cold hell for the buildings that house the scientists.

36
00:02:54,001 --> 00:02:58,380
People knew that buildings had a very short lifespan at pole.

37
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:02,160
The snow starts accumulating as soon as you put something on the surface.

38
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:04,800
Mother nature tries to cover up that scar,

39
00:03:05,220 --> 00:03:08,540
and it'll drift very quickly right to the top of the structure.

40
00:03:10,720 --> 00:03:16,720
By the time the 1957 buildings were replaced in 1975 for the geodesic dome structure,

41
00:03:17,920 --> 00:03:21,840
the first base was buried under 25 feet of ice and snow.

42
00:03:21,841 --> 00:03:25,420
the dome did not fare much better

43
00:03:25,421 --> 00:03:27,060
the dome

44
00:03:27,061 --> 00:03:28,480
that was like Humpty Dumpty

45
00:03:28,481 --> 00:03:31,320
once it got snow on the side of it

46
00:03:31,321 --> 00:03:32,880
the bottom ring cracked

47
00:03:32,881 --> 00:03:34,620
the pieces started coming apart

48
00:03:34,621 --> 00:03:36,920
it was impossible to put it back together again

49
00:03:36,921 --> 00:03:41,760
the challenge was to come up with a design

50
00:03:41,761 --> 00:03:43,320
that could extend building life

51
00:03:43,321 --> 00:03:44,480
past 20 years

52
00:03:44,481 --> 00:03:46,820
while accommodating a growing population

53
00:03:46,821 --> 00:03:48,760
250 people

54
00:03:48,761 --> 00:03:51,680
almost 10 times that of the original station

55
00:03:51,681 --> 00:03:58,680
In 1992, the design task went to Ferraro, Choi and Associates.

56
00:03:58,681 --> 00:04:05,680
They came up with an 80,000 square foot, two-story, 150 million dollar station

57
00:04:05,681 --> 00:04:10,680
to be elevated three meters above the snow on columns that extend down to a raft foundation.

58
00:04:10,681 --> 00:04:15,680
A giant raft that moves with the slowly drifting ice.

59
00:04:15,681 --> 00:04:20,680
The building's windward face is shaped like an airplane wing

60
00:04:20,681 --> 00:04:25,660
to compress the wind so that it speeds up and pushes blown snow past the structure.

61
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:32,220
But after a while, the whole plateau is growing in height of snow, about 8 inches a year.

62
00:04:32,840 --> 00:04:36,300
And at some point in time, our building's going to be down inside of a bowl.

63
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:41,860
And then the snow will start closing that bowl up and drifting on top of the station.

64
00:04:41,861 --> 00:04:45,420
At this point, the new South Pole station will be jacked up,

65
00:04:45,421 --> 00:04:48,980
raised in small increments by hydraulic jacks

66
00:04:48,981 --> 00:04:51,960
until it is once again three meters above the snow.

67
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:56,040
This process can be executed twice.

68
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:02,360
But the intense cold is just as much a design obstacle as the rising snow.

69
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:07,620
The station's external doors are eight-inch thick heated refrigerator doors.

70
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:14,500
You get condensation built up on that interface between the door and the wall,

71
00:05:14,501 --> 00:05:18,180
and if it's not heated, you might not be able to open that door up.

72
00:05:18,420 --> 00:05:23,760
It's an ice bond, and egress out of a building is critical.

73
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:30,100
In this environment, nothing can be taken for granted, especially the pace of work.

74
00:05:30,520 --> 00:05:35,300
Whether it's doing a welding job or erecting a structure for a building,

75
00:05:35,301 --> 00:05:39,320
a little more than two and a half times as long there as it would here.

76
00:05:40,100 --> 00:05:41,880
Machines are compromised as well.

77
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:45,640
The hydraulic cranes, essential for heavy lifting,

78
00:05:46,160 --> 00:05:49,880
are not reliable once the temperature drops to minus 40 degrees Fahrenheit.

79
00:05:51,260 --> 00:05:53,380
We're still limited by temperature.

80
00:05:54,040 --> 00:06:00,740
When we get below minus 40, operationally there becomes some problems.

81
00:06:00,741 --> 00:06:07,140
And when you're flying a heavy piece of steel, you don't want to have operational problems.

82
00:06:08,820 --> 00:06:11,260
Selecting building materials was also a problem.

83
00:06:11,880 --> 00:06:15,580
Nothing is tested for that type of temperature.

84
00:06:16,460 --> 00:06:20,040
So our design temperature was 100 below zero Fahrenheit.

85
00:06:21,040 --> 00:06:23,460
So that was a gigantic obstacle.

86
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:30,840
The project, originally slated for completion in 2005, is a couple of years behind schedule.

87
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:35,980
But since the structure's design is modular, it's being built in stages,

88
00:06:36,680 --> 00:06:39,780
and individual modules are occupied as soon as they're complete.

89
00:06:41,880 --> 00:06:48,020
As obstacles are overcome, a new South Pole station rises above the snow and ice.

90
00:06:48,760 --> 00:06:52,840
The current station represents the most ambitious undertaking by any nation

91
00:06:52,841 --> 00:06:56,720
to establish an above-surface facility in Antarctic conditions.

92
00:06:59,820 --> 00:07:02,460
And how long will this latest pole station last?

93
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:06,880
If the building lasted 40 years, if I'm still around, I'd be very happy.

94
00:07:06,881 --> 00:07:14,980
Success for buildings in the Antarctic ice and snow is temporary.

95
00:07:16,120 --> 00:07:23,240
At the opposite end of the world, the preeminent craftsmen of the cold, the Eskimos, perfected the temporary structure.

96
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:28,640
For the Eskimos, shelter from harsh winds and cold was a matter of life and death.

97
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:33,420
But building materials in these barren wildernesses were scarce.

98
00:07:33,421 --> 00:07:38,260
They made winter dwellings out of what was at hand.

99
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:43,100
Bone, snow, stone, driftwood, and even sod.

100
00:07:43,720 --> 00:07:48,860
Sod is a kind of very thick, packed earth that has a lot of plant material in it.

101
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:51,560
It's ideal for insulating, for warmth.

102
00:07:52,660 --> 00:07:56,620
But it's the snow house for which the Eskimos are best known.

103
00:07:57,160 --> 00:08:00,980
In part of the Canadian Arctic, there was no building material,

104
00:08:00,981 --> 00:08:06,780
and the people needed to be out on the edge of the ice in order to hunt seals for a great deal of the winter.

105
00:08:07,820 --> 00:08:11,660
Though the word igloo encompasses all forms of houses in Eskimo culture,

106
00:08:12,420 --> 00:08:16,540
the word is typically misunderstood to refer only to the snow house.

107
00:08:18,600 --> 00:08:23,880
The snow houses were temporary shelters and could be built by two men in the span of an hour.

108
00:08:23,881 --> 00:08:28,080
using a snow knife to cut blocks from packed snow.

109
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:31,900
The men would then stack the blocks into a small domed structure.

110
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:37,000
The dwelling was further insulated by packing snow on the outside.

111
00:08:39,080 --> 00:08:41,640
Inside, sleeping platforms were elevated

112
00:08:41,641 --> 00:08:44,540
to reach the warmer air trapped toward the top of the dome.

113
00:08:45,460 --> 00:08:48,720
And sometimes they made small cities out of these.

114
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:51,660
Several groups would come together out on the ice.

115
00:08:51,661 --> 00:08:54,600
the villages would vanish with a spring thaw

116
00:08:54,601 --> 00:08:56,580
and the community would move on

117
00:08:56,581 --> 00:08:58,560
having survived another winter

118
00:08:58,561 --> 00:09:04,860
in contemporary Alaska

119
00:09:04,861 --> 00:09:07,820
the Eskimos' minimalist approach to shelter

120
00:09:07,821 --> 00:09:10,020
has given way to high-tech solutions

121
00:09:10,021 --> 00:09:11,100
to beating the cold

122
00:09:11,101 --> 00:09:13,720
during the winter time

123
00:09:13,721 --> 00:09:15,560
it can be 30, 40 degrees below zero

124
00:09:15,561 --> 00:09:17,840
that's almost as cold as it is in the stratosphere

125
00:09:17,841 --> 00:09:19,180
we're right at the

126
00:09:19,181 --> 00:09:21,600
end of habitability

127
00:09:21,601 --> 00:09:23,600
when it comes to insulation.

128
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:32,400
So we typically see something on the order of 7 to 10 inches of fiberglass insulation

129
00:09:32,401 --> 00:09:35,440
and walls being an economic amount to include.

130
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:39,620
But it's just as vital to protect the permafrost,

131
00:09:40,100 --> 00:09:43,100
the permanently frozen ground below, from the house's heat,

132
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:46,140
as it is to protect the house from the cold.

133
00:09:46,141 --> 00:09:52,340
Any house part that comes in contact with the soil is going to lose heat to the soil,

134
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:56,380
and that might degrade the permafrost and cause a foundation failure.

135
00:09:57,540 --> 00:10:01,640
Melting the permafrost can turn a firm foundation into unstable ground.

136
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:06,700
Some of it has segregated ice that are just big wedges of ice

137
00:10:06,701 --> 00:10:10,600
that when they thaw leave a huge gap and it can subside feet.

138
00:10:10,601 --> 00:10:17,000
Elena and Joseph Ritter chose to build a top ground that's frozen to a depth of 40 feet,

139
00:10:17,660 --> 00:10:23,000
partly because a lot with permafrost is cheaper, but also because of the site's remoteness.

140
00:10:23,960 --> 00:10:29,640
We both really enjoy the idea of having all this space and wilderness right around us.

141
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:33,980
Their house rests on an aluminum triodetic space frame,

142
00:10:34,980 --> 00:10:40,520
a grid braced by horizontal and vertical diagonals that form a rigid floating slab.

143
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:45,960
This is the standard for any building on permafrost.

144
00:10:45,961 --> 00:10:48,340
You want to keep a three-foot airspace underneath.

145
00:10:48,341 --> 00:10:53,480
You know, you don't want to create any hindrance to natural convection, keeping the air flowing under the building.

146
00:10:54,080 --> 00:11:00,240
It's made to be perfectly rigid by the use of the three-dimensional construction.

147
00:11:01,260 --> 00:11:07,140
This corner has totally subsided out from under the house, and there's no drywall cracking or any damage inside the house.

148
00:11:07,141 --> 00:11:12,440
But if putting down roots in the cold is this demanding, why do it?

149
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:15,960
It's sort of an explorer mentality. It's a challenge mentality.

150
00:11:16,120 --> 00:11:18,660
A lot of people are just intrigued by that.

151
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:22,380
Whether it's for buildings or for bodies,

152
00:11:23,240 --> 00:11:26,120
comfort in the extreme cold depends on insulation.

153
00:11:27,020 --> 00:11:31,380
And the line between comfort and frostbite can be the thickness of your parka.

154
00:11:31,381 --> 00:11:39,460
In 1997, Russia's Vostok Station in Antarctica reported the coldest temperature in the world,

155
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:42,020
minus 132 degrees Fahrenheit.

156
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:46,580
This is colder than dry ice and cold enough to make steel shatter.

157
00:11:47,340 --> 00:11:49,880
Sub-Zero will return on Modern Marvels.

158
00:11:52,780 --> 00:11:53,480
Exposure

159
00:11:53,481 --> 00:11:58,520
If you're a runway model or a sun worshiper, you can't live without it.

160
00:11:58,521 --> 00:12:05,520
But if you're in the cold, or worse yet, the extreme cold, you can't live for long with it.

161
00:12:05,521 --> 00:12:10,520
The human body has a built-in strategy for dealing with excessive exposure to the cold.

162
00:12:10,521 --> 00:12:20,498
If the body's core temperature is threatened, it starts sacrificing the extremities, limiting blood flow to hands, feet, and even the face in an effort to protect its

163
00:12:20,499 --> 00:12:21,520
core temperature.

164
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:28,520
This can lead to frostbite, gangrene, and if you're exposed for too long, death.

165
00:12:28,521 --> 00:12:32,520
Sub-zero environments aren't for the faint-hearted.

166
00:12:32,521 --> 00:12:34,324
50 below is a real thing.

167
00:12:34,325 --> 00:12:39,520
You can go from being fine to 10 minutes later having frozen fingertips.

168
00:12:39,521 --> 00:12:44,520
I mean, just rock-hard, frozen things you cannot do anything with.

169
00:12:44,521 --> 00:12:48,520
At South Pole, I noticed that the water in my eyes started freezing.

170
00:12:48,521 --> 00:12:49,221
freezing.

171
00:12:49,222 --> 00:12:54,280
The scientist I was with had his parka down and within about 10 minutes the skin on his

172
00:12:54,281 --> 00:12:59,709
cheeks started turning black. And this is about 80 some odd degrees below zero.

173
00:12:59,710 --> 00:13:00,740
So the wind and

174
00:13:00,741 --> 00:13:04,082
the cold can really kill you very quickly.

175
00:13:04,083 --> 00:13:08,220
The coldest place on earth, Antarctica, has featured

176
00:13:08,221 --> 00:13:10,352
prominently in tales of survival.

177
00:13:10,353 --> 00:13:14,680
Perhaps the most famous and tragic was the 1911 competition between

178
00:13:14,681 --> 00:13:18,600
the British and the Norwegians to be the first to reach the South Pole.

179
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:22,236
Led by Robert F.

180
00:13:22,237 --> 00:13:27,440
Scott, the British team fell victim to poor planning, inferior gear, and

181
00:13:27,441 --> 00:13:33,560
ultimately, the weather. Having exhausted themselves pulling their own sleds, they

182
00:13:33,561 --> 00:13:38,740
arrived second at the pole, and dispirited, perished in a snowstorm on their return.

183
00:13:38,741 --> 00:13:47,080
By contrast, the Norwegians, led by Roald Amundsen, a consummate cold-weather tactician,

184
00:13:47,820 --> 00:13:54,440
were first to the pole on December 14, 1911, in what was a carefully planned, almost uneventful journey.

185
00:13:58,940 --> 00:14:07,200
The key to Amundsen's success was that he understood dog driving, sleds, the proper cold-weather diet, and fur clothing,

186
00:14:07,201 --> 00:14:10,960
because he had lived with and studied the Eskimos in the Arctic.

187
00:14:11,760 --> 00:14:16,840
He knew firsthand that the Eskimos had proven cold weather techniques and technologies.

188
00:14:18,120 --> 00:14:25,140
One thing that's very distinctive about Eskimo people is this hugely inventive technology.

189
00:14:25,141 --> 00:14:29,340
They just seem to be able to adapt to any circumstances.

190
00:14:29,341 --> 00:14:41,372
Hundreds of years ago, Eskimos were already using many of the same clothing strategies to stay warm that we use today, including creating wind-resistant and waterproof

191
00:14:41,373 --> 00:14:42,073
garments.

192
00:14:44,040 --> 00:14:50,460
Waterproof boots in some areas were made in an incredibly ingenious way.

193
00:14:50,640 --> 00:14:57,720
When a seal was taken, it was gutted in such a way that all of the insides came out through the mouth.

194
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:02,240
A container was then made from the skin of the seal.

195
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:09,140
These seal pokes, as the containers were known, were filled with seal oil, which was used for cooking and heating.

196
00:15:10,220 --> 00:15:16,540
And then after the seal poke, after several years had gotten very, very saturated with oil,

197
00:15:17,140 --> 00:15:23,340
the women would slit it in two and turn it inside out and generally de-hair them

198
00:15:23,341 --> 00:15:27,260
and make boots out of this pre-soaked material.

199
00:15:29,340 --> 00:15:34,400
The resultant boot was water-resistant and would be used out on the ice.

200
00:15:38,340 --> 00:15:40,880
Snow goggles were carved out of wood and ivory.

201
00:15:42,040 --> 00:15:44,240
The narrow ice lift served a dual purpose,

202
00:15:45,040 --> 00:15:47,920
both to protect the eyes from glare and prevent snow blindness,

203
00:15:48,540 --> 00:15:52,640
and to encourage the wearer to squint, which gives better distance vision.

204
00:15:54,600 --> 00:15:55,800
Good dogs.

205
00:15:55,801 --> 00:16:02,800
Today's clothing is still about staying dry and staying insulated from the cold.

206
00:16:02,801 --> 00:16:05,800
The whole way insulation works is trapping air.

207
00:16:05,801 --> 00:16:08,800
You don't heat the fiber, you're heating the air.

208
00:16:08,801 --> 00:16:14,800
A really warm jacket, if you've got two inches of insulation, here's your skin.

209
00:16:14,801 --> 00:16:21,800
It'll move that freezing point out to right near the edge of the outer fabric on your garment.

210
00:16:21,801 --> 00:16:32,120
Because when you're going from 80 above inside your garment to 50 below, you've got a 130 degree Fahrenheit temperature change in two inches.

211
00:16:32,620 --> 00:16:39,100
Though down and wool are still common insulators, the most prevalent insulating fiber is polyester fleece.

212
00:16:40,020 --> 00:16:46,800
In 1981, Aaron Fjordstein and his staff at Malden Mills pioneered a new manufacturing process for this product.

213
00:16:46,801 --> 00:16:52,360
By precision shearing both sides of the knit polyester to the same length,

214
00:16:52,900 --> 00:16:56,620
they were able to turn fleece into a durable, warm, man-made insulator.

215
00:16:58,040 --> 00:17:00,600
Moisture reduces the effectiveness of any insulator,

216
00:17:01,020 --> 00:17:04,380
and polyester fleece has proven efficient at drawing perspiration,

217
00:17:04,820 --> 00:17:07,340
which lowers body temperature away from the body.

218
00:17:08,120 --> 00:17:10,580
But fleece alone doesn't solve the problem.

219
00:17:11,180 --> 00:17:14,980
If you're working up a sweat, you need to realize that you are,

220
00:17:14,981 --> 00:17:17,980
and before you do that, you need to take layers off.

221
00:17:19,680 --> 00:17:23,820
Once you've got your insulation, you'll need a good outer shell to cut the wind.

222
00:17:24,440 --> 00:17:28,060
50 below is such a magical temperature because you can take some fabrics out

223
00:17:28,061 --> 00:17:32,880
that are rock solid at room temperature, 70 above.

224
00:17:33,360 --> 00:17:36,440
You take them out at 40 below, 50 below, and you can rip them like paper.

225
00:17:37,260 --> 00:17:41,460
This kind of gear testing is essential for people whose lives depend on it.

226
00:17:44,980 --> 00:17:48,660
like professional dog musher, Allie Zirkle.

227
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:52,720
When you're out on a sled, you know, 85 miles from the nearest village,

228
00:17:52,820 --> 00:17:54,620
nowhere near any kind of generator source,

229
00:17:54,840 --> 00:17:57,820
you have to stay warm or you're going to be in a tricky situation.

230
00:18:00,520 --> 00:18:02,980
Allie's cold weather attire is carefully chosen

231
00:18:02,981 --> 00:18:05,580
and has all been extensively field tested.

232
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:09,200
When she's dressing for competition in the cold,

233
00:18:09,640 --> 00:18:11,660
Allie's underlayer starts with wool shorts,

234
00:18:11,661 --> 00:18:16,260
followed by polyester long underwear, wool socks, and polyester wrist warmers.

235
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:20,500
Her next layer consists of sleeveless expedition weight bibs with a fleece top.

236
00:18:20,980 --> 00:18:26,000
Layer 3 consists of a pair of neck gaiters, a fleece jacket, and a mid-layer pair of nylon pants.

237
00:18:26,480 --> 00:18:32,500
Her outer layer contains Gore-Tex shell wind pants, a minus 50 Fahrenheit parka with a beaver or wolf ruff,

238
00:18:33,420 --> 00:18:37,560
an anorak nylon shell, breathable wool boots, and fleece gloves.

239
00:18:38,240 --> 00:18:43,360
The finishing touches are a pair of overshoes, beaver mitts, a beaver hat, and a headlamp.

240
00:18:47,160 --> 00:18:50,640
Her dog team comes by their insulation with a little less effort.

241
00:18:53,040 --> 00:18:57,080
Alaskan huskies have a double coat, kind of the same thing as a wolf has.

242
00:18:57,081 --> 00:19:02,860
It's an underlayer of a thick, furry layer, and then the outside layer is guard hairs.

243
00:19:03,120 --> 00:19:06,960
So that's why these dogs can live out in 40 below and do all right.

244
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:12,920
The dogs themselves haven't changed much over the years, but the sleds have.

245
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:19,260
Early Eskimo materials, including wood and bone held together with caribou hide,

246
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:25,280
have been replaced by lighter and more durable aircraft aluminum, held together with nuts and bolts.

247
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:31,700
Allie's sled is packed to be lightweight, but to efficiently carry the obvious.

248
00:19:32,680 --> 00:19:36,340
Sleeping bags, dry change of clothes, dog food, stove.

249
00:19:36,341 --> 00:19:38,940
and the not-so-obvious.

250
00:19:39,100 --> 00:19:40,600
Most of the time in the Arctic, it's real dark.

251
00:19:41,040 --> 00:19:43,860
This is just one of the headlights that I actually carry.

252
00:19:44,020 --> 00:19:46,260
When I go out on a long race, 1,000-mile race,

253
00:19:46,261 --> 00:19:48,580
I'll probably have two or three backup headlights

254
00:19:48,581 --> 00:19:51,360
and some pretty hardcore lithium batteries.

255
00:19:51,800 --> 00:19:55,060
Obviously, staying warm is the issue.

256
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:56,960
We do use a lot of chemical heat warmers.

257
00:19:57,820 --> 00:19:59,360
We'll pack quite a few of those.

258
00:20:01,320 --> 00:20:04,040
These heat packs are activated by opening the package

259
00:20:04,041 --> 00:20:05,800
and exposing its contents to air.

260
00:20:06,340 --> 00:20:10,200
The warmers contain chemicals that oxidize iron and release heat.

261
00:20:12,540 --> 00:20:15,900
Staying warm and dry in the extreme cold is half the battle.

262
00:20:16,740 --> 00:20:19,640
But getting around successfully is the other half.

263
00:20:20,380 --> 00:20:24,740
If you have to travel in sub-zero conditions, ice can be a real killer.

264
00:20:27,080 --> 00:20:30,260
Synthetic fleece is made from finely spun plastic fibers.

265
00:20:31,160 --> 00:20:36,240
Approximately 25 2-liter plastic bottles can be recycled to make one fleece jacket.

266
00:20:36,340 --> 00:20:39,760
Sub-Zero will return on Modern Marvels.

267
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:46,460
In cold conditions, there's a subtle boundary that's quite real.

268
00:20:47,280 --> 00:20:48,840
It can be found at zero.

269
00:20:50,040 --> 00:20:52,680
Zero is the liminal point between positive and negative.

270
00:20:53,680 --> 00:20:59,860
And on the Celsius scale, it's the line between water as a liquid and as a solid, ice.

271
00:21:00,220 --> 00:21:02,280
It's tough to solve icing problems.

272
00:21:02,920 --> 00:21:04,960
It's an unusual material. It's tenacious.

273
00:21:05,780 --> 00:21:07,840
Ice sticks to everything so well.

274
00:21:08,520 --> 00:21:11,600
It expands as it freezes, so it destroys things.

275
00:21:12,260 --> 00:21:18,200
So it's a wonderful, beautiful, and exceedingly dangerous and difficult to deal with problem.

276
00:21:19,660 --> 00:21:25,660
The line at which water turns into ice is also the line at which transportation becomes treacherous.

277
00:21:26,660 --> 00:21:32,220
Halloween 1994, a commuter aircraft, an aerospace shell ATR-72,

278
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,080
was in a holding pattern over Indiana.

279
00:21:35,560 --> 00:21:37,760
And during that time, they were icing.

280
00:21:38,100 --> 00:21:41,660
The de-icing boots were causing ice to pop off the wing.

281
00:21:42,500 --> 00:21:44,320
But when you have a large amount of liquid water,

282
00:21:44,420 --> 00:21:48,340
the conditions are warm and the droplets are large, it freezes slowly.

283
00:21:48,341 --> 00:21:50,680
The plane was equipped with de-icing boots,

284
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:53,840
inflatable rubber strips on the leading edge of the wing

285
00:21:53,841 --> 00:21:57,060
that fill with air and break off accumulated ice.

286
00:21:57,520 --> 00:22:01,980
But water ran aft, froze, and formed a ridge behind the boots.

287
00:22:02,880 --> 00:22:05,780
The ice buildup didn't impair the plane until its descent,

288
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:07,200
when the flaps were raised.

289
00:22:08,060 --> 00:22:09,120
Airflow was altered,

290
00:22:09,560 --> 00:22:12,120
and a jump in the aerodynamic flow over the wings

291
00:22:12,121 --> 00:22:15,500
affected the control surfaces, the ailerons.

292
00:22:15,980 --> 00:22:18,240
It was going to cause something called aileron snatch,

293
00:22:18,360 --> 00:22:20,440
where aileron popped up,

294
00:22:20,940 --> 00:22:23,240
and the airplane then went into a deep roll.

295
00:22:23,840 --> 00:22:26,320
I think it was a 70 degree roll.

296
00:22:26,540 --> 00:22:30,240
They corrected it and finally it rolled over on its back and went down those first.

297
00:22:37,580 --> 00:22:41,920
Millions of dollars a year are spent de-icing planes and helicopters on the ground

298
00:22:41,921 --> 00:22:47,260
with propylene glycol, a freeze point depressant that melts existing ice

299
00:22:47,261 --> 00:22:50,380
and lowers the temperature at which additional water will freeze.

300
00:22:50,381 --> 00:22:59,920
Propylene glycol is sprayed on aircraft at very high temperatures, usually around 170 degrees Fahrenheit.

301
00:23:00,420 --> 00:23:07,100
But one jet can require several thousand gallons of de-icing fluid at the cost of $7 a gallon.

302
00:23:07,600 --> 00:23:10,080
So it costs a lot of money to de-ice an airplane.

303
00:23:11,700 --> 00:23:16,160
But there are promising, less expensive alternatives, like infrared de-icing,

304
00:23:16,161 --> 00:23:23,200
which uses the heat from infrared light, long wavelength light, not visible to the eye, to melt off the ice.

305
00:23:23,940 --> 00:23:28,040
But it takes longer to de-ice with infrared technology than it does with glycol.

306
00:23:31,100 --> 00:23:34,240
Icing is an equally dangerous transportation problem in the ocean.

307
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:39,340
But in this case, it's the weight, not the shape of the ice, that's the issue.

308
00:23:41,160 --> 00:23:45,620
That ship bow plunges into the waves, you create a giant cloud up over the ship.

309
00:23:46,160 --> 00:23:49,380
And that water freezes on the ship if the temperature is cold enough.

310
00:23:50,460 --> 00:23:53,720
And because the ice is forming up above the center of gravity,

311
00:23:54,160 --> 00:23:59,340
as that ship rolls, it rolls more slowly and it rolls farther each time.

312
00:24:00,020 --> 00:24:01,200
Further and further.

313
00:24:01,620 --> 00:24:06,480
Eventually, if enough ice secretes, the ship will roll and it doesn't recover.

314
00:24:06,900 --> 00:24:07,840
And it goes over.

315
00:24:10,420 --> 00:24:13,780
The technique for de-icing a ship is an old-fashioned one

316
00:24:13,781 --> 00:24:17,360
that removes the ice without doing much damage to the ship's paint job.

317
00:24:17,820 --> 00:24:21,600
The way it's been done traditionally and the way it's done today is with baseball bats.

318
00:24:22,260 --> 00:24:24,320
And not any old baseball bat.

319
00:24:24,760 --> 00:24:26,300
It's a wooden baseball bat.

320
00:24:27,140 --> 00:24:28,660
Ship icing is unpredictable.

321
00:24:29,420 --> 00:24:31,160
But some ice events are a given.

322
00:24:32,140 --> 00:24:36,040
Each winter, fast ice, sea ice that is attached to land,

323
00:24:36,560 --> 00:24:40,380
forms in the Ross Sea and blocks the resupply of McMurdo Station,

324
00:24:40,381 --> 00:24:44,040
the central transportation hub of the U.S. Antarctic program.

325
00:24:44,660 --> 00:24:51,280
The ice can extend anywhere from McMurdo from a low of about 15 miles to upwards of 80 to 90 miles,

326
00:24:51,700 --> 00:24:55,840
and we have to break a channel through that ice to get those resupply ships in and out.

327
00:24:56,900 --> 00:25:01,440
The task of breaking through to resupply, called Operation Deep Freeze,

328
00:25:02,240 --> 00:25:05,760
falls to the two strongest conventionally powered icebreakers in the world.

329
00:25:05,761 --> 00:25:12,760
The Coast Guard's Polar Sea and Polar Star, both commissioned in 1976.

330
00:25:12,761 --> 00:25:16,800
We have a combination power plant of turbine engines that produce a tremendous amount of

331
00:25:16,801 --> 00:25:20,520
horsepower and diesel engines for normal cruising.

332
00:25:20,521 --> 00:25:25,760
Our turbine engines can produce 60,000 horsepower with a burst up to 75,000 horsepower.

333
00:25:25,761 --> 00:25:31,760
It gives us the ability to ride up on the ice, the weight of the bow falls down, the

334
00:25:31,761 --> 00:25:34,920
flotation of the stern actually lifts up and we kind of get a hammering effect.

335
00:25:36,660 --> 00:25:42,580
The polar icebreakers can continuously cut through ice to a depth of six feet at three knots.

336
00:25:44,920 --> 00:25:48,500
Typically, each vessel makes the Antarctic journey in alternating years

337
00:25:48,501 --> 00:25:51,500
and begins breaking the channel around Christmas Day.

338
00:25:53,160 --> 00:25:58,540
But recently, because iceberg B-15A shifted its position,

339
00:25:58,541 --> 00:26:01,820
blocking currents and causing a buildup of sea ice.

340
00:26:02,820 --> 00:26:04,460
Both vessels were on hand

341
00:26:04,461 --> 00:26:07,460
to ensure that the vital resupply of McMurdo Station

342
00:26:07,461 --> 00:26:09,220
was completed by mid-February.

343
00:26:13,700 --> 00:26:15,360
From McMurdo Station,

344
00:26:15,800 --> 00:26:18,680
supplies are flown 850 miles onward

345
00:26:18,681 --> 00:26:20,040
to the South Pole Station

346
00:26:20,041 --> 00:26:22,600
on LC-130 cargo planes.

347
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:26,640
The National Science Foundation

348
00:26:26,641 --> 00:26:28,420
is attempting to find an alternative

349
00:26:28,421 --> 00:26:30,900
to this costly link in the transportation chain

350
00:26:30,901 --> 00:26:33,860
by charting a trail between the two bases.

351
00:26:35,660 --> 00:26:37,780
An overland traverse provides the opportunity

352
00:26:37,781 --> 00:26:43,080
to deliver non-perishable goods with tractors and sleds,

353
00:26:43,500 --> 00:26:44,440
mainly fuel,

354
00:26:45,340 --> 00:26:48,100
and it's very inefficient using aircraft to deliver fuel.

355
00:26:49,040 --> 00:26:52,060
For every three gallons of fuel an aircraft burns,

356
00:26:52,220 --> 00:26:54,080
they deliver about two gallons of fuel.

357
00:26:54,081 --> 00:27:02,180
begun in 2002 the South Pole Travers covers a 1,000 mile path from McMurdo

358
00:27:02,181 --> 00:27:11,060
Station on Ross Island to the South Pole the path crosses the Ross ice shelf heads

359
00:27:11,061 --> 00:27:15,560
up the trans-antarctic mountains via the Leverett glacier and then continues a

360
00:27:15,561 --> 00:27:22,280
final 150 miles to the South Pole we have 85,000 pound vehicles traveling from

361
00:27:22,281 --> 00:27:26,120
from McMurdo to the South Pole, so safety is a primary concern.

362
00:27:27,280 --> 00:27:33,780
The trail is riddled with crevasses, splits in the ice ranging from 70 to 120 feet deep,

363
00:27:33,781 --> 00:27:36,780
which are deadly because they're not readily visible.

364
00:27:36,781 --> 00:27:40,780
Their surfaces are bridged by a thick covering of snow.

365
00:27:40,781 --> 00:27:46,780
As a result, the trail had to be prospected both by helicopters and snowmobiles,

366
00:27:46,781 --> 00:27:50,280
using ground-penetrating radar to chart a generally safe path.

367
00:27:50,281 --> 00:27:56,680
Our technique is to use dynamite and basically expose a hole big enough for a mountaineer to drop inside,

368
00:27:56,800 --> 00:28:01,040
get the actual internal dimensions so we can determine a volume we need to fill it.

369
00:28:01,980 --> 00:28:03,760
Fill, in this case, is snow.

370
00:28:04,420 --> 00:28:07,880
The longest part of the entire task wasn't filling the crevasses.

371
00:28:07,881 --> 00:28:11,480
It was actually identifying safe areas for the bulldozers to operate.

372
00:28:13,740 --> 00:28:18,740
But it's not only in Antarctica that safe transportation at low temperatures is challenging.

373
00:28:19,680 --> 00:28:25,380
It's really difficult to describe just what happens to an automobile

374
00:28:25,381 --> 00:28:28,720
or to almost anything in the world at 40 degrees below zero.

375
00:28:28,880 --> 00:28:33,880
Lubricants become stiff, or motor oil becomes stiff, batteries become weak.

376
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:38,140
In cities like Fairbanks, Alaska, when it gets cold enough,

377
00:28:38,680 --> 00:28:41,740
drivers plug in the engine warmers beneath their car's hoods

378
00:28:41,741 --> 00:28:46,080
so that fluids and batteries will stay warm, or their cars won't start.

379
00:28:46,081 --> 00:28:52,380
One local company is marketing a retractable cord reel to make this job just a little easier in the cold.

380
00:28:53,000 --> 00:28:55,940
The Arctic Leach provides 110 voltage to the vehicle.

381
00:28:56,640 --> 00:28:58,500
Within the vehicle, there's an adapter.

382
00:28:58,940 --> 00:29:01,220
We have a cord that goes to a battery blanket.

383
00:29:01,660 --> 00:29:05,360
We have an additional one that goes underneath the engine to the oil pan,

384
00:29:05,361 --> 00:29:09,400
and then one that goes into the engine block that keeps the engine warm.

385
00:29:09,880 --> 00:29:15,100
Granted, it's just an extension cord, but it's the only all-weather retractable cord reel in the world.

386
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:20,080
Snow and ice do present a daunting challenge for transportation,

387
00:29:20,081 --> 00:29:25,080
but there have always been people who can't wait to get out on this sea of white.

388
00:29:28,080 --> 00:29:34,080
If you want to go in the snow, the snowmobile is the vehicle of choice for work and playing.

389
00:29:34,081 --> 00:29:40,080
I have a cabin that's three miles from a snow park, so we rely on the sleds to get in.

390
00:29:40,081 --> 00:29:45,080
We have utility companies that buy the snowmobiles to get to remote cell sites.

391
00:29:46,080 --> 00:29:58,140
Though early motorized snow vehicles date back to the 1930s, it wasn't until the 1950s, with the introduction of smaller gasoline engines, that one- and two-passenger

392
00:29:58,141 --> 00:30:01,700
snowmobiles were sold and recreational use began.

393
00:30:05,240 --> 00:30:08,700
Polaris came out with their first snowmobiles in 1954.

394
00:30:08,701 --> 00:30:16,920
Today, four manufacturers will sell over 200,000 snowmobiles in the United States, Canada and Europe.

395
00:30:18,700 --> 00:30:27,480
In remote areas lacking roads and clouds, dog sleds have been replaced by snowmobiles as the functional vehicle of the backcountry.

396
00:30:28,640 --> 00:30:33,740
A snowmobile's spinning track propels the vehicle and keeps it aloft in the snow.

397
00:30:34,740 --> 00:30:37,080
The skis provide steering and additional lift.

398
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:41,180
Today's sleds pack quite a bit of punch.

399
00:30:41,660 --> 00:30:46,300
World record speeds on snowmobiles is right around 150 miles an hour.

400
00:30:46,960 --> 00:30:49,800
You can get them up and over a lot of terrain,

401
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:55,540
but easily, consistently, 40-degree slopes that they'll climb up.

402
00:30:57,160 --> 00:30:59,020
Comfort has come a long way as well.

403
00:30:59,540 --> 00:31:05,800
We've got things called hot doggers or little ovens that you can put on your exhaust pipe,

404
00:31:06,140 --> 00:31:08,480
put food in them, warm the food up.

405
00:31:08,700 --> 00:31:12,900
A hot meal is always welcome in the cold.

406
00:31:12,901 --> 00:31:16,900
But in the Arctic, a little heat can be a dangerous thing.

407
00:31:16,901 --> 00:31:24,400
On the Trans-Alaska Pipeline, permafrost gets a little help to stay permanently frozen.

408
00:31:24,401 --> 00:31:29,940
During Operation Deep Freeze's yearly resupply of Antarctica's science stations, 8 million

409
00:31:29,941 --> 00:31:35,920
gallons of fuel and 11 million pounds of cargo are delivered to McMurdo Station.

410
00:31:35,921 --> 00:31:38,920
Sub-Zero will return on Modern Marvels.

411
00:31:41,140 --> 00:31:45,300
Throughout the world, ice appears in myriad shapes and forms.

412
00:31:46,240 --> 00:31:50,740
But where industry and infrastructure are concerned, ice's role is not so varied.

413
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:56,520
It can be cleanly divided into two camps, ally or obstacle.

414
00:31:58,620 --> 00:32:01,660
In the case of Alieska's Trans-Alaska Pipeline,

415
00:32:02,420 --> 00:32:04,980
hundreds of miles of ice, frozen in the ground,

416
00:32:04,981 --> 00:32:09,800
were one of the primary obstacles to moving 14 billion barrels of oil

417
00:32:09,801 --> 00:32:12,660
from Prudhoe Bay to the Port of Valdez.

418
00:32:14,240 --> 00:32:19,440
The permanently frozen ground that the Trans-Alaska Pipeline traversed

419
00:32:19,441 --> 00:32:24,700
had to be traversed in a manner that would not allow the permafrost

420
00:32:24,701 --> 00:32:27,940
to degrade to the point to where it would become soft

421
00:32:27,941 --> 00:32:30,940
and would no longer support the pipeline.

422
00:32:30,941 --> 00:32:39,640
One of the solutions was to build half the pipeline, over the 400 miles of permafrost affected area, above ground,

423
00:32:40,280 --> 00:32:44,540
separating the hot oil in the insulated pipe from the icy foundation below it.

424
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:55,220
The liquid is about 104 degrees coming out of Prudhoe Bay, and it is about 30 degrees colder by the time it arrives in Valdez.

425
00:32:55,221 --> 00:33:01,400
But insulation and separation weren't enough to ensure that the permafrost would stay frozen.

426
00:33:04,020 --> 00:33:09,240
Alieska's engineers turned to thermosiphons, closed pipes with refrigerant inside.

427
00:33:10,500 --> 00:33:17,120
The 120,000 thermosiphons on the pipeline remove heat from the soil and transfer it to the air.

428
00:33:17,121 --> 00:33:27,040
The heat in the permafrost evaporates the ammonia liquid and the ammonia sends the column.

429
00:33:27,740 --> 00:33:36,900
Then in the wintertime, at the very top of the heat pipe, the ammonia condenses into a liquid and rejects its heat to the atmosphere.

430
00:33:37,480 --> 00:33:43,160
The air temperature must be below the temperature of the permafrost, 31 degrees Fahrenheit,

431
00:33:43,780 --> 00:33:50,120
to be cold enough to condense the ammonia vapor and return it to soil level so that the cycle can continue.

432
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:55,120
As a result, this passive system only operates in winter.

433
00:33:56,720 --> 00:34:03,380
So the concept is that you want to remove as much heat as you possibly can in the wintertime

434
00:34:03,381 --> 00:34:08,160
so that it can carry through that frozen ground to the end of summer.

435
00:34:10,180 --> 00:34:12,300
Elsewhere in the National Petroleum Reserve,

436
00:34:12,940 --> 00:34:17,000
23.5 million acres of public land on Alaska's North Slope,

437
00:34:18,180 --> 00:34:21,580
ice is an ally rather than an obstacle to oil exploration.

438
00:34:24,240 --> 00:34:28,120
Companies like Peak Oil Field Service build ice roads,

439
00:34:28,960 --> 00:34:31,800
roads made of shaved ice from lakes and sprayed water,

440
00:34:31,801 --> 00:34:35,920
to take oil rig components out to remote areas on the North Slope.

441
00:34:36,860 --> 00:34:40,780
There's a tremendous environmental benefit to building an ice road

442
00:34:40,781 --> 00:34:43,440
as opposed to a traditional gravel road.

443
00:34:44,340 --> 00:34:48,120
For one thing, you utilize resources around you, freshwater lakes.

444
00:34:48,820 --> 00:34:52,340
You use snow and ice chips off of the lakes.

445
00:34:52,341 --> 00:34:56,140
And then when it's all said and done, it simply melts.

446
00:34:58,060 --> 00:35:01,020
The ice roads provide routes to little-known areas

447
00:35:01,021 --> 00:35:04,460
where companies are drilling exploration or wildcat wells.

448
00:35:06,500 --> 00:35:11,320
So for a company to go out there and not really know what's there,

449
00:35:11,480 --> 00:35:15,040
it's not economical to build a gravel road 60 miles out

450
00:35:15,041 --> 00:35:17,300
and then come up with nothing.

451
00:35:18,460 --> 00:35:20,960
The process begins with an initial pass

452
00:35:20,961 --> 00:35:23,280
in which the route gets its first coat of water.

453
00:35:25,160 --> 00:35:27,940
The next step is similar to standard road construction.

454
00:35:28,480 --> 00:35:36,740
We'll get onto lake surfaces and we will trim ice and we will gather snow and haul it conventionally,

455
00:35:36,920 --> 00:35:41,880
similar to a gravel construction, but we'll be using either ice aggregate or snow.

456
00:35:42,440 --> 00:35:47,240
Each time we put the ice aggregate or snow in place, we'll add water to it

457
00:35:47,241 --> 00:35:52,440
and we'll use our graters and blade it to form the template that we want.

458
00:35:52,441 --> 00:35:58,720
Trucks carrying more than 6,000 gallons of water cap the road with a smooth coating of water.

459
00:35:59,300 --> 00:36:00,700
It quickly becomes ice.

460
00:36:01,660 --> 00:36:05,880
The finished product is a road capable of carrying surprisingly heavy loads.

461
00:36:06,280 --> 00:36:11,160
We've carried loads in the neighborhood of about 1 to 1.5 million pounds.

462
00:36:11,680 --> 00:36:13,880
And these loads are rigged modules.

463
00:36:14,740 --> 00:36:18,680
This last week, our temperatures were probably in the average of minus 25.

464
00:36:18,681 --> 00:36:25,260
you have a tremendous amount of traction on that ice when the temperatures are that cold.

465
00:36:27,520 --> 00:36:31,520
Of course, in the springtime and as the sun starts to come up more and more,

466
00:36:31,640 --> 00:36:33,720
it's like standing on a skillet. I mean, it's slippery.

467
00:36:35,360 --> 00:36:39,960
And that slippery ice is their signal that it's time to leave the road and let it melt away.

468
00:36:42,920 --> 00:36:45,560
While most people look forward to a spring thaw,

469
00:36:46,320 --> 00:36:48,960
For some communities, melting ice can be devastating.

470
00:36:50,740 --> 00:36:55,200
An ice-blocked river can flood in moments after a spring rain or snow melt.

471
00:36:56,320 --> 00:36:58,360
The ice cover will break up and run downstream,

472
00:36:58,980 --> 00:37:04,240
and then you can get ice jams in the river where the ice transport capacity of the river

473
00:37:04,241 --> 00:37:09,020
isn't sufficient to carry the ice, or the moving ice hits an intact ice cover.

474
00:37:09,500 --> 00:37:13,780
What happens is the upstream water levels rises, and it can cause overbank flooding.

475
00:37:13,781 --> 00:37:17,980
We have techniques for weakening the ice downstream.

476
00:37:18,200 --> 00:37:21,860
If we know the ice run will come down and it will collide with an intact ice cover.

477
00:37:22,140 --> 00:37:27,860
If we can weaken that ice cover, we may let the ice go right through the area where the flood will occur.

478
00:37:28,380 --> 00:37:32,320
And in that case, we may cover the ice with dark material so that it melts faster.

479
00:37:32,740 --> 00:37:36,880
Or we have techniques for going out on the ice and cutting slots, drilling holes,

480
00:37:36,980 --> 00:37:39,420
and other means for weakening the ice ahead of time.

481
00:37:39,421 --> 00:37:45,920
At the Army's Cold Regions Research and Engineering Laboratory in Hanover, New Hampshire,

482
00:37:46,780 --> 00:37:48,940
river ice control begins in the lab.

483
00:37:50,080 --> 00:37:55,220
We actually have models now that will model each individual piece of the ice jam.

484
00:37:55,340 --> 00:37:59,960
They're called discrete element models, and they may have many thousands of ice pieces,

485
00:38:00,220 --> 00:38:04,200
and we'll model each individual piece as an ice jam forms in the computer,

486
00:38:04,201 --> 00:38:10,340
and we can see what the impact is on the upstream stages and on the flows from this type of model.

487
00:38:11,680 --> 00:38:15,560
Sticky problems like river ice often receive complex solutions.

488
00:38:16,380 --> 00:38:22,040
But out in space, the Cassini spacecraft solves its issue with the cold in a simple fashion.

489
00:38:22,600 --> 00:38:25,360
It hides behind a warm blanket.

490
00:38:26,740 --> 00:38:31,160
In 1975, the Trans-Alaska Pipeline's peak construction year,

491
00:38:31,161 --> 00:38:34,340
over 28,000 were employed by Alieska.

492
00:38:35,200 --> 00:38:37,280
In the two years it took to build the pipeline,

493
00:38:38,060 --> 00:38:40,120
31 were killed in accidents.

494
00:38:41,280 --> 00:38:44,140
Sub-Zero will return on Modern Marvels.

495
00:38:47,140 --> 00:38:50,660
Two agencies oversee much of the U.S. science initiative

496
00:38:50,661 --> 00:38:52,180
that takes place in the cold.

497
00:38:53,080 --> 00:38:56,600
The Office of Polar Programs at the National Science Foundation

498
00:38:56,601 --> 00:38:57,900
and NASA.

499
00:38:58,860 --> 00:39:00,420
Their jurisdictions are respectively

500
00:39:00,421 --> 00:39:04,460
the polar regions here on Earth and the universe.

501
00:39:05,620 --> 00:39:10,220
Since Antarctica is remote and cold, it's often used as an analog for space.

502
00:39:11,900 --> 00:39:15,940
Temperatures outside the South Pole Station, minus 100 degrees Fahrenheit,

503
00:39:16,300 --> 00:39:21,060
and outside the International Space Station, minus 140 degrees Fahrenheit,

504
00:39:21,460 --> 00:39:23,260
are close enough for discomfort.

505
00:39:25,600 --> 00:39:28,380
Fueled by energy from an array of solar panels,

506
00:39:28,381 --> 00:39:32,540
the station's electronic equipment generates the heat that keeps it warm.

507
00:39:36,260 --> 00:39:40,300
In addition, the station is insulated by a highly reflective blanket

508
00:39:40,301 --> 00:39:42,660
made of mylar and Dacron

509
00:39:42,661 --> 00:39:46,940
that rejects solar radiation while preventing cold from penetrating.

510
00:39:49,840 --> 00:39:53,040
Such blankets can also be found on the Cassini spacecraft,

511
00:39:53,440 --> 00:39:58,280
which was launched in 1997 for a seven-year, billion-mile journey

512
00:39:58,281 --> 00:40:03,380
to study Saturn, where temperatures average minus 300 degrees Fahrenheit.

513
00:40:06,660 --> 00:40:10,020
Cassini's thermal blankets, consisting of 24 layers,

514
00:40:10,580 --> 00:40:13,700
including aluminized Kapton, Mylar, and Dacron,

515
00:40:14,280 --> 00:40:17,220
cover the three-story tall spacecraft from top to bottom.

516
00:40:18,500 --> 00:40:23,600
Typically the way it's done is you'll take sheets of brown patterning paper,

517
00:40:23,601 --> 00:40:26,820
and they tape this up to the actual spacecraft hardware,

518
00:40:26,821 --> 00:40:30,080
and they're bending it around and making nips and tucks,

519
00:40:30,400 --> 00:40:34,220
and then they'll take rolls of this metallized reflective foil

520
00:40:34,221 --> 00:40:36,680
and just keep stacking the material upon one another

521
00:40:36,681 --> 00:40:41,720
and then sew the edges together to produce a durable multi-layer insulation blanket.

522
00:40:43,040 --> 00:40:47,980
Before leaving Earth, Cassini was temperature tested at NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory.

523
00:40:49,060 --> 00:40:51,980
You're standing in front of JPL's 25-foot space simulator.

524
00:40:52,240 --> 00:40:55,500
It does cryogenic temperatures down to liquid nitrogen temperature,

525
00:40:55,501 --> 00:40:57,900
which is minus 185 degrees centigrade.

526
00:40:59,960 --> 00:41:02,460
After completing its long journey to Saturn,

527
00:41:03,360 --> 00:41:08,580
Cassini released its probe, Huygens, on Christmas Eve 2004.

528
00:41:10,780 --> 00:41:14,840
The probe has sent back data and photos of Saturn's moon, Titan,

529
00:41:15,620 --> 00:41:18,320
frozen at minus 290 degrees Fahrenheit.

530
00:41:18,321 --> 00:41:25,320
Another unmanned probe has been going through its paces here on Earth.

531
00:41:25,321 --> 00:41:38,320
On January 24, 2004, a balloon-shaped robot explorer, the Tumbleweed Rover, completed a successful eight-day, 40-mile wind-driven trip across Antarctica.

532
00:41:38,321 --> 00:41:47,320
The craft's mobility didn't shock its designers, since the concept for the rover was derived from a wind-related accident on another craft.

533
00:41:47,321 --> 00:41:50,320
They were doing some testing out in Mojave Desert.

534
00:41:50,321 --> 00:41:52,320
While they were out there, the wind started to pick up.

535
00:41:52,321 --> 00:41:55,320
One of the wheels broke off the three-wheeled rover,

536
00:41:55,321 --> 00:41:57,320
and they had a hard time chasing this thing.

537
00:41:57,321 --> 00:41:59,320
And that's where they had their idea.

538
00:41:59,321 --> 00:42:03,320
Wow, why don't we just instrument one of the wheels

539
00:42:03,321 --> 00:42:05,320
and see what we can do with it.

540
00:42:06,320 --> 00:42:10,320
The result of this accident was an 88-pound, 6-foot-tall inflatable

541
00:42:10,321 --> 00:42:16,320
that can carry a variety of instruments in a PVC pipe that spans its diameter.

542
00:42:17,320 --> 00:42:21,060
The instruments gathered topographical and environmental information.

543
00:42:21,960 --> 00:42:25,760
The rover may one day roll across the frozen surface of Mars,

544
00:42:26,400 --> 00:42:29,100
but first it had to pass cold tests on Earth.

545
00:42:30,860 --> 00:42:32,640
Warmed by the heat of its own electronics,

546
00:42:33,580 --> 00:42:37,220
the rover kept its internal instrument payload at 86 degrees Fahrenheit,

547
00:42:37,780 --> 00:42:42,900
while the temperature of its Antarctic surroundings averaged minus 22 degrees Fahrenheit.

548
00:42:43,860 --> 00:42:47,240
At the opposite end of the world, in the cold but fragile Arctic,

549
00:42:47,320 --> 00:42:51,880
An international team of scientists working with the National Science Foundation

550
00:42:51,881 --> 00:42:55,440
is attempting to understand warming trends in Arctic waters.

551
00:42:56,280 --> 00:42:58,960
I like to think of the analogy of a heart.

552
00:42:59,740 --> 00:43:04,200
Heat gets pumped up to the Arctic and fresh water gets pumped down.

553
00:43:04,920 --> 00:43:10,380
And exactly where that fresh water in the form of river water and ice melt ends up,

554
00:43:10,820 --> 00:43:12,720
back down in the Atlantic Ocean,

555
00:43:12,721 --> 00:43:16,260
has a strong impact on how the ocean circulates and on our climate.

556
00:43:17,320 --> 00:43:22,200
In addition to floating buoys that transmit data from the moving ice pack,

557
00:43:22,820 --> 00:43:25,500
scientists have installed a mooring in a fixed position,

558
00:43:26,120 --> 00:43:28,660
stretching 2.5 miles from the ocean floor.

559
00:43:30,540 --> 00:43:34,320
17 instruments have been attached to the line, including current meters,

560
00:43:35,480 --> 00:43:38,500
sensors to measure ocean temperature and salinity,

561
00:43:38,980 --> 00:43:42,260
and at the top, sonar to measure the draft of the ice.

562
00:43:42,261 --> 00:43:53,280
The big changes are basically that the water coming in from the Atlantic Ocean is a lot warmer than had ever been seen before by several degrees.

563
00:43:54,320 --> 00:44:00,400
And the waters just beneath the pack ice are changing as well, exposing the ice to increased temperatures.

564
00:44:02,520 --> 00:44:07,460
Just as we sometimes suffer in the cold, we will certainly suffer in its absence.

565
00:44:07,461 --> 00:44:10,700
the globe's climate is a delicate balancing act

566
00:44:10,701 --> 00:44:14,280
and it's through close observation of these coldest of places

567
00:44:14,281 --> 00:44:17,440
that we truly measure the vital signs of the planet

568
00:44:17,441 --> 00:44:22,020
and it's through our direct experience of the cold

569
00:44:22,021 --> 00:44:24,540
when we work in it, inhabit it

570
00:44:24,541 --> 00:44:26,200
and play in it

571
00:44:26,201 --> 00:44:29,200
that we can appreciate its icy gifts

